                "target_scores": {
                    "Looking at the mass spectrometry data, I can see several peaks with different m/z values and intensities. The most intense peak (100% intensity) is at m/z 233.96319960361987.\n\nLet me analyze the isotope pattern:\n- m/z 231.97 (intensity 77.1%)\n- m/z 233.96 (intensity 100%) - base peak\n- m/z 235.96 (intensity 24.4%)\n\nThis isotope pattern is characteristic of a compound containing chlorine or bromine. The spacing of ~2 mass units and the relative intensities suggest the presence of chlorine atoms.\n\nFor chlorine:\n- Cl-35 (75.76%) and Cl-37 (24.24%)\n\nThe pattern shows:\n- M peak at 231.97\n- M+2 peak at 233.96 (base peak)\n- M+4 peak at 235.96\n\nThis pattern is consistent with a compound containing 2 chlorine atoms. For two chlorines:\n- Both Cl-35: relative intensity ~57%\n- One Cl-35, one Cl-37: relative intensity ~100% (2 combinations)\n- Both Cl-37: relative intensity ~15%\n\nThe base peak at 233.96 suggests this is likely [M+H]+ or [M-H]- ion in ESI mode.\n\nIf 233.96 is [M+H]+, then M = 232.96\nIf 233.96 is [M-H]-, then M = 234.96\n\nLooking at the pattern more carefully, the peak at 231.97 with 77% intensity and 233.96 with 100% intensity suggests we're looking at M and M+2 peaks of a dichlorinated compound.\n\nLet me consider: if the molecular weight is around 232-234, and it contains 2 Cl atoms (2 x 35 = 70), that leaves 162-164 for the rest of the molecule.\n\nCommon molecular formulas with 2 Cl and MW ~232:\n- C6H4Cl2O2: 6(12) + 4(1) + 2(35.5) + 2(16) = 72 + 4 + 71 + 32 = 179 (too low)\n- C7H4Cl2O3: 7(12) + 4(1) + 2(35.5) + 3(16) = 84 + 4 + 71 + 48 = 207 (too low)\n- C8H4Cl2O4: 8(12) + 4(1) + 2(35.5) + 4(16) = 96 + 4 + 71 + 64 = 235 (close!)\n\nLet me recalculate more precisely with exact masses:\nC8H4Cl2O4: 8(12.000) + 4(1.008) + 2(34.969) + 4(15.999) = 96 + 4.032 + 69.938 + 63.996 = 233.966\n\nThis matches the base peak at 233.96! This would be the M peak with both Cl-35 isotopes.\n\nThe molecular formula is C8H4Cl2O4.": 0.0,
                    "Looking at the mass spectrometry data, I can see several peaks with different m/z values and intensities. The most intense peak (100% intensity) is at m/z 233.96319960361987.\n\nLet me analyze the isotope pattern:\n- m/z 231.97 (intensity 77.1%)\n- m/z 233.96 (intensity 100%) - base peak\n- m/z 235.96 (intensity 24.4%)\n\nThis isotope pattern is characteristic of a compound containing bromine. Bromine has two naturally occurring isotopes: ^79Br (50.69%) and ^81Br (49.31%), which are nearly equal in abundance.\n\nThe pattern shows:\n- M peak at 231.97 (with ^79Br)\n- M+2 peak at 233.96 (with ^81Br) - this is the base peak\n- M+4 peak at 235.96 (with two ^81Br or other heavy isotopes)\n\nThe presence of a strong M+2 peak suggests one bromine atom. The M+4 peak at ~24% intensity suggests the presence of two bromine atoms (since for two Br atoms, the M+4 peak would be about 25% of the base peak).\n\nLet me calculate the molecular weight. If we take the base peak at m/z 233.96, this likely represents [M+H]+ or [M-H]- depending on ionization mode. In ESI, both positive and negative modes are possible.\n\nFor a dibrominated compound:\n- Two Br atoms: 2 x 79.9 = 159.8\n- Remaining mass: 233.96 - 159.8 = 74.16 (if this is M+H+, then M = 232.96)\n- Or if M+2 is the molecular ion: M = 233.96\n\nLet me consider common molecular formulas with two bromines:\n- C2H2Br2O2: 2(12) + 2(1) + 2(79.9) + 2(16) = 24 + 2 + 159.8 + 32 = 217.8 (too low)\n- C3H4Br2O2: 3(12) + 4(1) + 2(79.9) + 2(16) = 36 + 4 + 159.8 + 32 = 231.8 ≈ 232\n\nThis matches! C3H4Br2O2 has a molecular weight of approximately 231.8-232, which fits with the observed m/z values.\n\nLet me verify: C3H4Br2O2\n- C: 3 x 12.011 = 36.033\n- H: 4 x 1.008 = 4.032\n- Br: 2 x 79.904 = 159.808\n- O: 2 x 15.999 = 31.998\nTotal = 231.871\n\nThis matches the m/z 231.97 peak (M+H+ with ^79Br,^79Br).": 0.0,
                    "Let me analyze the mass spectrometry data more carefully.\n\nThe peaks show:\n- m/z 233.96 (intensity 100) - BASE PEAK\n- m/z 235.96 (intensity 24.4) - isotope peak\n- m/z 231.97 (intensity 77.1)\n\nThe isotope pattern with peaks at 233.96 and 235.96 separated by 2 mass units with a ratio of about 100:24 strongly suggests one chlorine atom (Cl-35:Cl-37 ratio is approximately 3:1 or 75:25).\n\nIf 233.96 is [M-H]- with Cl-35, then the neutral molecule M = 234.96\n\nLet me systematically check formulas around mass 235:\n\nFor C10H5ClO4:\nExact mass = 10(12.000) + 5(1.008) + 34.969 + 4(15.999) = 120 + 5.04 + 34.969 + 63.996 = 224.005\nThis is too low.\n\nFor C10H4ClO5:\nExact mass = 10(12.000) + 4(1.008) + 34.969 + 5(15.999) = 120 + 4.032 + 34.969 + 79.995 = 238.996\n[M-H]- = 237.996 (too high by ~4)\n\nFor C9H4ClO5:\nExact mass = 9(12.000) + 4(1.008) + 34.969 + 5(15.999) = 108 + 4.032 + 34.969 + 79.995 = 226.996\n[M-H]- = 225.996 (too low by ~8)\n\nThere's a gap. Let me consider if there's nitrogen:\n\nFor C9H4ClNO4:\nExact mass = 9(12.000) + 4(1.008) + 34.969 + 14.003 + 4(15.999) = 108 + 4.032 + 34.969 + 14.003 + 63.996 = 225.000\n[M-H]- = 224.000 (too low)\n\nFor C10H4ClNO4:\nExact mass = 10(12.000) + 4(1.008) + 34.969 + 14.003 + 4(15.999) = 120 + 4.032 + 34.969 + 14.003 + 63.996 = 237.000\n[M-H]- = 236.000 (close!)\n\nFor C9H5ClNO4:\nExact mass = 9(12.000) + 5(1.008) + 34.969 + 14.003 + 4(15.999) = 108 + 5.04 + 34.969 + 14.003 + 63.996 = 226.008\n[M-H]- = 225.008 (too low)\n\nFor C10H5ClNO3:\nExact mass = 10(12.000) + 5(1.008) + 34.969 + 14.003 + 3(15.999) = 120 + 5.04 + 34.969 + 14.003 + 47.997 = 222.009\n[M-H]- = 221.009 (too low)\n\nFor C9H4ClNO5:\nExact mass = 9(12.000) + 4(1.008) + 34.969 + 14.003 + 5(15.999) = 108 + 4.032 + 34.969 + 14.003 + 79.995 = 240.999\n[M-H]- = 239.999 (too high)\n\nFor C10H3ClNO4:\nExact mass = 10(12.000) + 3(1.008) + 34.969 + 14.003 + 4(15.999) = 120 + 3.024 + 34.969 + 14.003 + 63.996 = 235.992\n[M-H]- = 234.992 (very close!)\n\nFor C9H3ClNO5:\nExact mass = 9(12.000) + 3(1.008) + 34.969 + 14.003 + 5(15.999) = 108 + 3.024 + 34.969 + 14.003 + 79.995 = 239.991\n[M-H]- = 238.991 (too high)\n\nC10H3ClNO4 gives [M-H]- = 234.992, which rounds to 234.99, very close to the observed 233.96!\n\nThe small difference (~1 mass unit) could be due to measurement precision or calibration. Given the isotope pattern clearly shows one Cl atom and the mass is very close, I believe the molecular formula is C10H3ClNO4.": 0.0,
